  <p>
    The specific line that is drawn through the scatter plot has this
    equation. 
    <md>
      \hat{y} = 41 + 0.59x
    <md>
    Remember that the hat notation over the dependent variable is
    there to remind us that this line is an approximation of the data,
    not a perfect fit to the data. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Now I can interpret the coefficients of this line. 41 is the
    intercept. On a raw interpretation, this would mean that if the
    explanatory variable were zero, the output would be 41. In our
    units, a total body length of 0cm predicts a head length of 41mm.
    This obviously doesn't make much sense: a body length of 0 is not
    actually a possum at all. However, the data here doesn't really
    care about (relatively) very small body lengths. The data is
    studying grown possums, not developmental data. So there is an
    interpretation here, but it's not much of a problem that the
    interpretation isn't very sensical. The dataset isn't really
    concerned with very small <m>x</m> values. (Of course, other data
    sets may care about the intercept, if the data does include values
    of the explanatory variable which are near zero.) 
  </p>
  <p>
    The slope coefficient is more useful here. The number <m>0.59</m>
    says that for each cm increase in body length, the expectation is
    that the corresponding length of the head of the possum is 0.59mm
    larger. In many ways, this coefficient is the relationship between
    the two variables, showing how changes in one variable are
    reflected in the other variable. 
  </p> 
  <p>
    A linear regression, once found, can be used to make predictions.
    If I put a body length into the equation of the line, say 90 cm,
    then the linear regression predicts a 94.1 millimeter long head.
    These predictions can then be compared against future data. In
    this way, a linear model is like a hypothesis: something that
    comes from the data but can be tested in future experiments to
    determine how well it holds up.
  </p>
  <p>
    As I have already noted, a linear model is an approximation. The
    data points are hopefully near the line, but not exactly on the
    line. That leads to the next definition.
  </p>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\hat y{y}</m> be a linear model of a data set. For each
        data point, the difference <m>y - \hat{y}</m> is called the
        <term>residual</term> of the data point. 
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <p>
    Using the previous example, consider the datapoint <m>(75,
    94.7)</m>. If I put x=75 into the line, it predicts 85.25 mm. The
    actual value is 84.7. The different, <m>y</m> minus
    <m>\hat{y}</m>, is the residual for this data point. In this case,
    the data is -.55 below the line: the residual is -0.55.
  </p>
  <p>
    Must can be done with residuals, and we will return to them in
    <xref ref="clo10-linear-analysis"/>. For now, though, I can use
    residuals to answer an important questions. I've talked about
    linear models, but I haven't yet said anything about how they are
    actually determined. How is the line and its coefficients
    discovered?
  </p>
  <p>
    There are several methods, but the most common is called the
    <term>method of least squares</term>. Let me sketch the idea.
    First, I can number all of the data points from 1 to <m>n</n>
    where <m>n</m> is the number of cases in the data set. 
    <md>
      (x_1, y_1), (x_2, y_2), \ldots, (x_n y_n)
    </md>
    Then, given any line <m>\hat{y} = b + mx</m> I can calculate the
    residuals of each data point. I'll use the letter <m>e</m> for
    these residuals (short for the error of the approximation). 
    <md>
      e_i = y_i - \hay{y}(x_i)  
    </md>
    This gives me a list of residuals, each measuring how far the data
    point is away from the line. 
    <md>
      e_1, e_2, \ldots e_n
    </md>
    Since the residuals measure the error, I want the error to be as
    small as possible. There are different ways to measure this, but
    the most common method looks at the sum of the squares of the
    residuals. 
    <md>
      e_1^2 + e_2^2 + \ldots + e_n^2
    </md>
    For any line, this sum of the squares of the residuals. I want the
    specific line for which this number is as small as possible. Such
    a line will be considered the best linear model. Since I have a
    bunch of squares and I want to minimie them, this is called the
    method of least squares.
  </p>
  <p>
    Why do we use squares? There are a number of reasons, some which
    require more mathematics to discussn. One important effect,
    though, is that it makes all the errors positive. Residuals can be
    positive or negative: above or below the line. By squaring them,
    I get a positive numbers for each error. A second important effect
    comes from the fact that squaring has a larger effect on larger
    numbers. Therefore, this method exagerated large errors: points
    which are very far away from the line. This method is quite
    sensitive to major outliers, which is desirable in this context. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Finally, let me talk about minimizing this sum. You might be
    wondering: how is this actually done? Sure, I can make this sum
    and say that I want a minimum value, but how is the minimum
    actually calculated? Unfortunately for us, calculating such a
    minimum is well beyond the mathematics expected in this course.
    This is a calculus problem, and a fairly difficult calculus
    problem at that. In general, mostly problems in mathematics that
    are searching for a minimum or a maximum end up being calculus
    problems. Since this is not a calculus course, I am not going to
    go into the minimization methods. 
  </p>
  <p>
    This covers the basic ideas of linear regression, but I want to
    introduce one more important idea in this section. The linear
    model so far had one explanatory variable. It is possible that we
    want to consider more than one explanatory variable. This leads to
    the idea of multiple linear regression. I'll state the definition
    specifically for three explanatory variables. 
  </p>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>y<m>, <m>x_1</m>, <m>x_2</m> and <m>x_3</m> be numeric
        variables ina  data set. A <term>multiple linear
        regression</term> is a linear model where <m>y</m> is the
        dependent variable and depends on all three of the other
        variables. It has this equation.
        <md>
          \hat{y} = b + m_1x_2 + m_2x_2 + m_3x_3
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <p>
    Those I have stated it for three variables, a multiple linear
    regression could have any number of variable. Notice that there is
    still only one intercept coefficient, but that each explanatory
    variable has its own slope coefficient. Each of those slope
    coefficients measures the way in which <m>y</m> depends on each of
    the explanatory variables. 
  </p>
  <p>
    I can no longer graphs a multiple regression, since there are too
    many variables (hence too many axes) to draw. But the principles
    are exactly the same: there are still residuals, I still can find
    a model by least squares, the coefficients still explains the
    relationship. I am just allowing multiple variables to have an
    effect on my dependent variable. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Let me go back to the example of this section. The data set also
    include variable for the age of the possum as well as the sex. I'd
    like to put these all in the linear model. Again, the head length
    is the dependent variable, which I will still call <m>y</m>. A
    multiple regression might look like this.
    <md>
      \hat{y} = 43.5 + 0.56(\text{length}) + 0.30(\text{age}) -
      1.66(\text{sex})
    <md>
    Then I can interpret these coefficients. As discussed above, the
    intercept is not terribly importnat here, but the other three are
    important. 0.56 means that each centimeter increase in total body
    length corresponds with a 0.56mm increase in head length. The 0.30
    say that each year older corresponds to 0.3mm increase in head
    length. Finally, there is also the sex variable. This requires
    some more discussion. 
  </p>
  <p>
    In this data set, unsurprisingly, sex is a nominal categorical
    varaible, with two categories: male and female. As such, I can't
    put it into an equation. However, there is way to solve this
    problem.
  </p>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        If I have a categorical variable in a dataset and I assign
        numbers to each category, the result is called an
        <term>indicator variable</term>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <p>
    The assignment of numbers usually arbitrary (though for ordinal
    categorical variable, we would usually try to preserve the natural
    order). In this case, the researches made this assignment. 
    <md>
      \text{male} \mapsto 0 \text{ and } \text{female} \mapsto 1.
    </md>
    This changes the categorical variable into numbers. Now I can
    interpret the last term in the multiple regression. The
    coefficient <m>-1.66</m> only takes effect for female possums. If
    the case is male, the indicator variable is 0, so this term is
    multiplied by 0 and has no effect. If the case is female, the
    indicator variable is 1, so the term subtract 1.66 from the head
    length. The result is the observation that the female possums of
    the same age and body length typically have a head length that is
    1.66mm shorter than the males. 
  </p>
  <p>
    I said I couldn't draw multiple regressions, since there are too
    many variables. However, I can often draw the effects of an
    indicator variable. Let me show you how. If I ignore the age
    category and just do regression with the body length and sex
    variables, I can treat each instance of the indicator variable as
    a new line. The coefficient <m>-1.66</m> move the line for female
    possums below the line for the male possums. <xref
    ref="figure-indicator-variable"/> show the result. In the figure,
    the cases are colour coded: red for male and blue for female. I
    get two lines with the same slope (the other slope coefficient is
    unchanged) but the lines are shifted up or down from each other.
    If I have more categories, I could likewise have a whole set of
    shifted lines. 
  </p>
